welcome back and let's bring in Ben  Shapiro editor-in-chief of The Daily  wire and host of the Ben Shapiro Show  podcast which I listen to every day Ben  I want to talk to you about this bill  that the President signed today but just  before the show started the  Congressional Budget Office put out this  number they are estimating a 12% drop in  GDP for this quarter forty percent  annualized and federal debt to exceed  GDP by the end of the year and I know  these are things you think about a lot  and maybe get your take on the president  signing this bill but what happens on  the other end of it I mean that is the  big question because the underlying  market fundamentals have changed I mean  the fact is that you can tell people  that they ought to stop being in  lockdown and it's still their consumer  behavior is going to change fairly  radically I think it's gonna be a while  before we have a therapeutic treatment  that lowers the risk of this thing if  you're above the age of 60 if you have  pre-existing conditions that describes a  large percentage of the population  you're just not gonna be eating in a  restaurant right next to somebody for a  long time you're not gonna be going to a  sporting event for a long time you're  probably not going to be in a retail  establishment that's highly crowded for  a very long time and that means that  actually it's more important not less  important to get people back to work so  that we can see how the markets play all  of this out so we can see which areas of  the economy are going to grow in sort of  the new demand world and people can move  from jobs that no longer exist to jobs  that will exist because the economy will  move in a different direction it's just  this is this is a sudden shock it's  gonna take a while for the economy to  recover it's not gonna be a v-shaped  recovery what do you think about the  tension between the the desire to reopen  and then sort the on the other side of  it some people including the media  looking out at them and saying it's too  soon don't be foolish and you're having  this real almost a cultural divide well  I think that a large part of what's  happening here is that Americans do  understand that or they should  understand that there is no situation  which everybody is absolutely safe  whether the threat comes to an end where  people go back to work and now they can  go without masks without social  distancing and the risk level is what it  was before any of this happened and I  think that that needs to be constantly  reinforced I also think that it's  important for politicians not to play  the the ridiculous game where they  suggest that if only we save a single  life then any policy is justified that's  the  last refuge of the political scoundrel  policymaking is always about weighing  competing values and weighing exactly  the cost to not only human liberty but  see human prosperity the number of lives  ruined not just the number of lives lost  what it looks like when you have 30  million people suddenly out of work  these are serious concerns and only  unserious people suggest that any policy  that is going to be taken is not going  to have costs and risks single factor  analysis here is the enemy of good sense  and that's why Andrew Cuomo is going to  end up opening up parts of New York  understanding that the risk still exists  for a lot of people that's just going to  happen the same thing will happen in  Illinois the same thing will happen in  California the question is how these  governor's assess that risk and exactly  what the current lockdown measures are  designed to do I think that very often  the media do a poor job explaining when  they said flatten the curve that was  designed to prevent the capacity of the  health care system from being exceeded  it was not designed to prevent everybody  from getting this in the first place  that just is not going to happen the the  testing regimens that people are talking  about those are designed to identify hot  spots to prevent spikes that again  prevent the healthcare system from being  overwhelmed they're not designed to  prevent huge numbers of people from  getting this over a long period of time  understanding that allows people to be  more sane in their risk assessment and  to be more rational about what exactly  society is going to look like coming out  of this because I think a too rosy and  optimistic picture of how safe we're  gonna be when this ends is a danger and  I think similarly a to pessimistic  version of what this looks like is also  going to be a danger there's also this  problem of that if you reopen and people  are so expected to go back to work but  the childcare centers and the schools  are still closed how our parents  supposed to make those decisions give  you the last word yeah I mean that  that's exactly right and again I think  that in the end we live in a republic  individual citizens are gonna have to  make decisions that don't threaten other  people but that allow them to live their  lives in a way that they feel  comfortable with and that risk  assessment is going to vary based on  person in a free country we're all going  to have to do that and to pretend  otherwise is simply just political line  ben shapiro thank you say hello to your  family and shabbat shalom  we'll see you hey thanks so much again  thank you
 

